### Bitcoin Knots and Bitcoin Core: rules ported into the Python

`xbt402/tx.py` `unified_sighash` is our port of Knots' unified signature hash, and
`tests/security/sechelp.py` `is_strict_der` is a port of Bitcoin Core's
`IsValidSignatureEncoding` (BIP 66, Pieter Wuille). Both are under the MIT licence above.

### libsecp256k1

Signing and verification of real keys goes through `coincurve`, which builds libsecp256k1
(<https://github.com/bitcoin-core/secp256k1>, MIT licence, Copyright (c) 2013 Pieter Wuille).
The pure-Python backend is a test oracle, not a constant-time implementation.

## Python libraries

The tests and the demos install the packages in `requirements.txt`: coincurve (MIT or
Apache-2.0), starlette, uvicorn and httpx (BSD-3-Clause). Two files need a package that
`requirements.txt` does not list: `xbt402/mcp_server.py` imports `mcp` (MIT) and
`scripts/sealed_key.py` imports `cryptography` (Apache-2.0 or BSD-3-Clause). None is vendored.
Their licence texts must accompany any distribution that includes them.

## Standards and prior work

Not code in this repository, but the design depends on them and the credit is theirs: Jeremy
Spilman's one-way payment channel (2013) and BIP 65; x402 (<https://github.com/x402-foundation/x402>,
Apache-2.0), created by Coinbase; BIP 66, 143, 173, 340 and 341; RFC 6979; Bitcoin Knots
(<https://github.com/bitcoinknots/bitcoin>) for the BLAKE2b proof of work (Luke Dashjr), the
unified signature hash and the reduced-data rules of BIP 110 (Dathon Ohm). References:
<https://github.com/AwokenLazarus/Lightning-Stream/blob/main/docs/REFERENCES.md>.
